MCP TOOL CONTRACT - PICONIX 2026-07-28 SUBSET

SERVER: mcpd & (TCP 8080 POST /mcp). Six tools. No auth or TLS;
use an isolated trusted LAN. A PTY and sh start with mcpd.
Only one HTTP client is processed at a time; PTY is shared.

TOOL SELECTION
  get_manual({})                  index (/man/README)
  get_manual({topic:'vi'})        one exact topic (see index)
  get_temperature({})             read board sensor
  execute_command({command:'ps'}) short noninteractive sh -c
  write_file({path:'/tmp/x',content:'ASCII'}) overwrite file
  terminal_read({})              read current PTY screen
  terminal_send({text:'vi /tmp/x'}) type ordinary characters
  terminal_send({key:'ENTER'})   send named key
Read actual tools/list argument schema; avoid invented fields.
Never use execute_command for vi, fd, reversi or full-screen apps.
See /man/terminal-operations before sending interactive input.

LIMITS AND PROCESS COST
  execute_command: <=119 ASCII command bytes, <=4096 output B,
  nominal wait ~3000 ms; each call spawns a shell and command.
  write_file: ASCII /tmp file, <=2048 B, overwrite only.
  Six user-process slots total, including mcpd and PTY sh.
  /man pages live in ROM, not RAM-file slots. Read one page/call.

SAFE NONINTERACTIVE PROGRAM EXAMPLE
  get_manual({topic:'languages/python'})
  write_file({path:'/tmp/demo.py',content:'print(42)\n'})
  execute_command({command:'python /tmp/demo.py'})
  Verify returned output and error status. Do not launch bare
  python via execute_command (interactive REPL).

INTERACTIVE PROGRAM EXAMPLE
  terminal_read({})                 # confirm shell prompt
  get_manual({topic:'fd'})
  terminal_send({text:'fd /tmp'})
  terminal_send({key:'ENTER'})
  terminal_read({})                 # inspect app state
  terminal_send({text:'q'})         # only if fd accepts it
  terminal_read({})                 # confirm return to sh

RELIABILITY
  terminal_send returns accepted byte count, not a screen or
  confirmation that a command completed. terminal_read may capture
  a partial redraw; read again if state remains transitional.
  On send/HTTP failure, inspect state; do not retry mutations or
  keys blindly. Shell exit triggers restart; a terminal_send
  detecting restart rejects the input without sending it.
  Inspect /man/safety-recovery for transport and retry rules.
